WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:05.680
You might call this a
modern Cinderella story.

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.580
But in our story,

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:09.630
the glass sneakers don't
find one pair of feet

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:12.360
to wear them happily ever after.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:14.620
Instead they become assets

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:17.003
which retain their value over time.

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:21.343
This modern retelling
is all about reselling.

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:25.360
There's no prince in this story,

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:27.793
but there are some very handsome profits.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:31.880
Vestiaire Collective is
a luxury resale company

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:34.620
whose story began in 2009,

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:36.500
when a group of chic Parisians

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:38.660
decided to clean out their closets

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:41.203
and sell on what they
weren't wearing anymore.

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:45.060
Now the fashion resale market is booming.

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:46.400
And earlier this year,

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:50.907
Vestiaire collective achieved
a valuation of $1.7 billion

00:00:50.907 --> 00:00:53.193
in its latest round of funding.

00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:57.680
But this is not the only
resale success story.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:00.360
Resale companies on both
sides of the Atlantic

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:04.390
are attracting investors
with a promise of new growth

00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:05.943
from old clothes.

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:10.360
And between Vestiaire's
buyers and sellers is this

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:14.570
the authentication center,
and it's never been busier.

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:16.260
The whole year of 2020,

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:19.150
we've seen a hundred percent
growth on items being listed.

00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:22.318
Just thinking about the sheer
scale of unused inventory

00:01:22.318 --> 00:01:23.653
is just enormous.

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:26.220
It's time to examine

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:29.693
the unstoppable rise of fashion resale.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:36.550
What we wear says a lot about who we are,

00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:40.800
yet fashion is also a $2.5
trillion global industry

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.943
that touches everyone on earth.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:46.040
I'm Imran Amed.

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:48.690
I first started trying to make
sense of the fashion business

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:52.770
15 years ago, as it was being
transformed by technology,

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:56.023
globalization and
shifting consumer values.

00:01:56.939 --> 00:01:58.860
Now I'm on a journey to see

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:02.150
how fashion is recalibrating
amid the pandemic,

00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:05.130
to balance profit with purpose.

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:07.270
This is the Business of Fashion show.

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:10.790
Join me to discover how fashion
shapes business, culture,

00:02:10.790 --> 00:02:12.390
and identity,

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:15.533
and to meet the people
forging fashion's future.

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:20.053
Old is the new new.

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:23.460
While style setters have long embraced

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:25.990
the chicest vintage clothes,

00:02:25.990 --> 00:02:29.390
and savvy shoppers search
thrift and secondhand stores

00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:32.800
on the promise of originality
and self-expression,

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:36.440
online resale platforms
are facilitating a bonafide

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:39.533
buy, sell, buy, sell boom.

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:44.399
Luxury brands thrive on strict
control of distribution,

00:02:44.399 --> 00:02:46.833
price and perception.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:51.670
Yet some are now embracing
resale to recruit new customers

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:54.423
and deepen engagement with existing ones.

00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:57.660
Right across the resale market,

00:02:57.660 --> 00:02:59.750
the simplicity of transactions

00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:02.480
combined with shifting customer values

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:06.150
means secondhand is taking up
more space in fashion closets

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:08.820
now and in the future,

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:13.160
as more and more of what we
love to wear is pre-loved.

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:14.530
And the sellers?

00:03:14.530 --> 00:03:18.340
In the US alone, it is
estimated that 9 billion items

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:20.620
of clothing are left unworn.

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:22.750
According to BOF Insights,

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:25.770
secondhand fashion sales
in the United States

00:03:25.770 --> 00:03:30.370
could top $67 billion by 2025.

00:03:30.370 --> 00:03:34.010
Buyers are first and foremost
seeking value for items

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:37.610
for which they would not be
prepared to pay full price.

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:40.920
While sustainability ranks
only third as a reason

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:42.263
to purchase resale.

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:48.810
Sellers and investors cite
support of circularity

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:51.660
and the eco cred of reducing emissions,

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:54.170
energy and water use.

00:03:54.170 --> 00:03:58.260
But should we really be
celebrating this as slow fashion

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:00.730
or is it just another quick fix?

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:04.290
Could reselling unwanted
items actually be accelerating

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:06.303
more first-hand consumption?

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:09.230
Drops in limited edition products

00:04:09.230 --> 00:04:11.540
create urgency and scarcity,

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:14.613
with some resell prices
more than six times higher

00:04:14.613 --> 00:04:17.260
than retail prices.

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:20.400
And how to be sure of real versus fake?

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:22.170
In the buzzy youth segment,

00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:24.972
how to protect peer-to-peer
teenage traders

00:04:24.972 --> 00:04:28.156
in exposed online communities?

00:04:28.156 --> 00:04:30.990
Resale platforms must be vigilant,

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:33.790
on authenticity and safety,

00:04:33.790 --> 00:04:36.740
while customers on both
sides of the transaction

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:39.810
should keep age old mottoes in mind:

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:43.060
'Caveat Emptor, Caveat Venditor',

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:45.453
let the buyer and the seller beware.

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:55.523
I'm on a train headed to Lille,
a few hours north of Paris.

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:00.910
It's here that Vestiaire Collective,

00:05:00.910 --> 00:05:02.920
the luxury resale company,

00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:05.410
has set up its authentication center

00:05:05.410 --> 00:05:08.793
to verify that the
products it sells are real.

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:15.883
CEO Max Bittner showed me around.

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:20.570
This whole area in
Tourcoing was a massive part

00:05:20.570 --> 00:05:22.070
of the garment industry,

00:05:22.070 --> 00:05:23.550
which then obviously got hit very hard

00:05:23.550 --> 00:05:24.983
with the whole outsourcing.

00:05:24.983 --> 00:05:25.816
Right.

00:05:25.816 --> 00:05:26.774
So the people here are super passionate,

00:05:26.774 --> 00:05:28.450
but they also really love fashion.

00:05:28.450 --> 00:05:29.670
It's really a fashion industry,

00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:32.460
so we can actually attract
good authenticators,

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:34.350
people who've been in the industry.

00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:38.523
In 2019, Max joined
Vestiaire Collective as CEO

00:05:38.523 --> 00:05:40.210
from Lazada,

00:05:40.210 --> 00:05:43.160
a Southeast Asian online shopping portal

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:45.390
acquired by Alibaba.

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:46.223
I mean,

00:05:46.223 --> 00:05:47.056
the huge difference here with us

00:05:47.056 --> 00:05:49.940
is the senior authenticators
and all the authenticators

00:05:49.940 --> 00:05:54.000
get three months training,
which is very long.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:56.370
And what's the training that's provided?

00:05:56.370 --> 00:05:57.840
We have the experts who've come here,

00:05:57.840 --> 00:05:59.620
whether they're from
auction houses or the like,

00:05:59.620 --> 00:06:01.190
and you're speaking to
one of them in a second.

00:06:01.190 --> 00:06:02.850
Right.

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:05.280
They basically go by brand, by category,

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:06.740
all the learnings that we've acquired

00:06:06.740 --> 00:06:08.400
over the last 12 years.

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:09.370
And with a lot of the brands,

00:06:09.370 --> 00:06:11.553
we've received also training from them.

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:14.580
I'm in charge of the authentication.

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:17.240
This is my seventh year
at Vestiaire Collective.

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:19.890
I come from fashion auctions.

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:22.420
A counterfeiter will always do a mistake.

00:06:22.420 --> 00:06:24.950
And our job is to detect the mistake.

00:06:24.950 --> 00:06:28.020
If there is a mistake, of
course, it's a counterfeit.

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:31.530
What's the most expensive item ever sold

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:32.740
on Vestiaire Collective?

00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:34.970
The most expensive item ever sold was

00:06:34.970 --> 00:06:37.220
an €85,000 Himalayan Birkin,

00:06:37.220 --> 00:06:42.220
which was sold from a Hong Kong
seller to a New York buyer.

00:06:42.700 --> 00:06:44.900
We actually had an incident two weeks ago

00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:48.780
where someone bought a
250,000 golden Patek Philippe,

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:50.340
but the seller then didn't ship it,

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:51.920
so we were very sad about that.

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:54.763
But there have been
items close to €100,000,

00:06:55.771 --> 00:06:57.450
bought and sold on the platform.

00:06:57.450 --> 00:06:58.960
The lower price point,

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:02.270
the minimum basket size
you can sell is €15.

00:07:02.270 --> 00:07:05.020
So you can't sell anything
which is below €15.

00:07:05.020 --> 00:07:06.510
If you think about the average basket size

00:07:06.510 --> 00:07:08.780
of some of these peer to peer platforms,

00:07:08.780 --> 00:07:10.180
I think that is the average basket size.

00:07:10.180 --> 00:07:12.280
So we clearly differentiate there.

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:16.730
And we try to limit what I
would call broadly fast fashion.

00:07:16.730 --> 00:07:18.240
We don't really want to have fast fashion

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:20.230
and encourage fast
fashion on the platform.

00:07:20.230 --> 00:07:21.650
Why not?

00:07:21.650 --> 00:07:24.380
We have a positioning of
more higher end fashion,

00:07:24.380 --> 00:07:26.630
real luxury, real designer,

00:07:26.630 --> 00:07:30.760
something that people want to buy and sell

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:31.970
for a much longer period.

00:07:31.970 --> 00:07:34.570
One of the key things in
the roles that we play,

00:07:34.570 --> 00:07:35.730
as a secondhand platform,

00:07:35.730 --> 00:07:37.150
is that we want to educate people

00:07:37.150 --> 00:07:40.220
that what they buy and
sell is not consumables,

00:07:40.220 --> 00:07:41.370
just fashion.

00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:42.500
It's really an asset.

00:07:42.500 --> 00:07:46.060
And if you think about the
way we, as a consumer class,

00:07:46.060 --> 00:07:49.370
need to change the way we
think about consumption,

00:07:49.370 --> 00:07:51.583
is that we need to take much better care

00:07:51.583 --> 00:07:54.410
and appreciate the value
of the items that we have

00:07:54.410 --> 00:07:56.460
because there is a residual value.

00:07:56.460 --> 00:07:59.170
How do you respond to
that kind of criticism,

00:07:59.170 --> 00:08:03.340
that the resell market
actually propagates consumption

00:08:03.340 --> 00:08:06.590
as opposed to manages it or minimizes it?

00:08:06.590 --> 00:08:09.030
I think that's probably the question

00:08:09.030 --> 00:08:10.440
that concerns us the most

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:12.842
because I think as part
of our core mission,

00:08:12.842 --> 00:08:16.470
we want to change consumer
behavior to the better.

00:08:16.470 --> 00:08:18.150
And we've actually spent
quite a lot of time

00:08:18.150 --> 00:08:21.420
with external agencies
to challenge ourselves.

00:08:21.420 --> 00:08:26.260
And our goal is to be
carbon neutral by '25,

00:08:26.260 --> 00:08:27.670
without any offsetting.

00:08:27.670 --> 00:08:31.030
And we've looked through all our emission,

00:08:31.030 --> 00:08:32.500
whether it's our transport

00:08:32.500 --> 00:08:34.820
or the way that we reduce consumption

00:08:34.820 --> 00:08:37.210
and we think that target
of being carbon neutral

00:08:37.210 --> 00:08:38.990
without offsetting is possible.

00:08:38.990 --> 00:08:40.420
The fact of the matter is,

00:08:40.420 --> 00:08:44.140
we do reduce the amount people consume

00:08:44.140 --> 00:08:47.610
because what we help them
to do is to trade up.

00:08:47.610 --> 00:08:50.280
Instead of buying Zara firsthand,

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:51.540
they buy Sézane secondhand,

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:53.340
if buying Sézane firsthand,

00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:54.770
they buy Yves Saint Laurent secondhand,

00:08:54.770 --> 00:08:57.530
instead buying firsthand Chanel,
they buy secondhand Hermès.

00:08:57.530 --> 00:08:59.600
So you basically go up the food chain

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:02.720
and we want to help and
educate every consumer

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:04.840
that they should maybe
pay a bit more upfront

00:09:04.840 --> 00:09:06.880
for an item that they're
going to wear longer

00:09:06.880 --> 00:09:08.570
and where they're more
likely to take care of it

00:09:08.570 --> 00:09:09.880
and then resell it in the future.

00:09:09.880 --> 00:09:12.311
You didn't come from this world.

00:09:12.311 --> 00:09:15.800
You probably didn't even know
what a Himalaya Birkin was.

00:09:15.800 --> 00:09:17.420
I used to climb the Himalayas, maybe,

00:09:17.420 --> 00:09:20.770
but I didn't find any Birkins there.

00:09:20.770 --> 00:09:25.470
No, I have to admit I'm a brute.

00:09:25.470 --> 00:09:27.670
I think there would be
many people in the industry

00:09:27.670 --> 00:09:29.700
who would probably still
roll their eyes at me.

00:09:29.700 --> 00:09:31.350
And that's fine.

00:09:31.350 --> 00:09:36.350
I made my career selling $6
dresses or $70 mobile phones

00:09:37.300 --> 00:09:39.200
across Indonesia and Malaysia.

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:40.910
It's a very different business.

00:09:40.910 --> 00:09:41.743
But in the end,

00:09:41.743 --> 00:09:44.760
what we do is use technology
to connect buyers and sellers.

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:48.140
It's about customer service,
it's about user experience.

00:09:48.140 --> 00:09:51.100
But the beautiful thing about
the products that we sell now,

00:09:51.100 --> 00:09:54.060
and I've really gained
massive appreciation to it,

00:09:54.060 --> 00:09:55.940
is there's real craftsmanship.

00:09:55.940 --> 00:10:00.930
They're real pieces of art
and trying to educate people

00:10:00.930 --> 00:10:04.330
of that underlying intrinsic
value of these items

00:10:04.330 --> 00:10:08.820
and therefore making
people change towards,

00:10:08.820 --> 00:10:11.230
what I think, is better consumption.

00:10:11.230 --> 00:10:14.470
I mean is probably the most
fulfilling thing there is.

00:10:14.470 --> 00:10:17.110
How has your customer behavior,

00:10:17.110 --> 00:10:20.090
both in terms of sellers and buyers,

00:10:20.090 --> 00:10:23.280
shifted over this period of disruption

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:24.510
that we've all been living through?

00:10:24.510 --> 00:10:27.540
It's really allowed people
to be quite self-reflective

00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:29.410
and to start asking themselves, okay,

00:10:29.410 --> 00:10:31.447
what can I do myself to be different?

00:10:31.447 --> 00:10:33.730
And secondhand is one
of these very few things

00:10:33.730 --> 00:10:35.480
where people can do something today.

00:10:35.480 --> 00:10:38.430
How do you manage that
tension around authenticity?

00:10:38.430 --> 00:10:43.033
Because without authenticity,
this luxury focused operation

00:10:44.106 --> 00:10:46.600
starts to teeter a bit.

00:10:46.600 --> 00:10:51.530
If you step back on the
actual topic of fakes,

00:10:51.530 --> 00:10:54.830
at the moment, one in
1000 products is fake.

00:10:54.830 --> 00:10:57.910
So the chances of buying
something on Vestiaire

00:10:57.910 --> 00:11:00.220
and it not being authentic is one in 1000.

00:11:00.220 --> 00:11:02.380
Is that perfect? No, it's not perfect.

00:11:02.380 --> 00:11:05.260
And we're by no means nearly done

00:11:05.260 --> 00:11:07.250
on what we can do to improve it.

00:11:07.250 --> 00:11:08.800
Coming back to the customers,

00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:10.827
we have this incredibly engaged community.

00:11:10.827 --> 00:11:12.993
And the beauty about secondhand is

00:11:12.993 --> 00:11:15.930
that people really define their own style.

00:11:15.930 --> 00:11:20.930
They can shop back decades to
find what they're looking for.

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:23.440
They can find products

00:11:23.440 --> 00:11:25.560
that they might not be able to look for.

00:11:25.560 --> 00:11:29.170
I mean, we often think the world is Paris,

00:11:29.170 --> 00:11:30.570
the world is London,
the world is New York,

00:11:30.570 --> 00:11:33.020
but we have access to people, to products,

00:11:33.020 --> 00:11:35.660
that would never get access
to anywhere in the world.

00:11:35.660 --> 00:11:39.023
So it's really about this
global digital wardrobe,

00:11:40.310 --> 00:11:42.340
which gives access to products

00:11:42.340 --> 00:11:44.280
that people might not have access to

00:11:44.280 --> 00:11:45.410
because they're too expensive

00:11:45.410 --> 00:11:48.113
or because they just don't have
a store anywhere near them.

00:11:49.152 --> 00:11:52.020
So I just get really excited about it.

00:11:52.020 --> 00:11:54.470
And I look at technology
and what it can provide.

00:11:55.481 --> 00:11:58.093
It's endless opportunity.

00:11:59.500 --> 00:12:01.700
The rise of resale now is akin to

00:12:01.700 --> 00:12:04.120
that of e-commerce 20 years ago.

00:12:04.120 --> 00:12:08.400
The field is wide open. The
front runner has yet to emerge.

00:12:08.400 --> 00:12:11.170
No wonder it is attracting
billions of dollars

00:12:11.170 --> 00:12:12.610
of investment.

00:12:12.610 --> 00:12:15.773
It's a similar story of
upswing over in London.

00:12:24.460 --> 00:12:27.670
So we went to Lille, we visited
with Vestiaire Collective

00:12:27.670 --> 00:12:30.110
to learn about the luxury
part of the resell market.

00:12:30.110 --> 00:12:33.180
Now we're here in the heart of east London

00:12:33.180 --> 00:12:35.300
to learn about the mass end of the market.

00:12:35.300 --> 00:12:36.920
We're here to meet with Depop.

00:12:36.920 --> 00:12:38.930
They are a technology company here,

00:12:38.930 --> 00:12:41.230
and they've had their own
journey during the pandemic.

00:12:41.230 --> 00:12:44.620
And we're here to meet
with their CEO Maria Raga.

00:12:44.620 --> 00:12:48.160
Depop is skewed towards lower
price products exchanged

00:12:48.160 --> 00:12:52.500
between younger traders, almost
all of them 26 and under,

00:12:52.500 --> 00:12:55.280
and who are attracted
to the community aspect

00:12:55.280 --> 00:12:57.923
that a peer to peer platform can offer.

00:12:59.740 --> 00:13:04.740
What makes a community
focused platform like Depop

00:13:05.070 --> 00:13:09.580
different from typical fashion e-commerce?

00:13:09.580 --> 00:13:12.060
From the very beginning,

00:13:12.060 --> 00:13:14.400
when you think about the way
the app has been designed,

00:13:14.400 --> 00:13:16.910
it has been designed with that in mind.

00:13:16.910 --> 00:13:19.450
It's not just a place where
you come to find an item

00:13:19.450 --> 00:13:20.283
that you want.

00:13:20.283 --> 00:13:21.116
It's a place where you come

00:13:21.116 --> 00:13:23.530
to discover other people
doing other things.

00:13:23.530 --> 00:13:26.680
Where you can actually
interact with the users,

00:13:26.680 --> 00:13:28.870
and then you can also engage
with them in real life.

00:13:28.870 --> 00:13:32.020
And so for us, having this
community-first appeal

00:13:32.020 --> 00:13:36.170
was extremely, extremely
important from the very beginning

00:13:36.170 --> 00:13:38.850
and it has translated into
how we build the product

00:13:38.850 --> 00:13:40.310
and also how we do marketing.

00:13:40.310 --> 00:13:43.210
There's a huge overlap
of buyers and sellers

00:13:43.210 --> 00:13:44.043
in the platform.

00:13:44.043 --> 00:13:46.723
And that really creates
that sense of community,

00:13:46.723 --> 00:13:50.280
it's not like a B-to-C model,

00:13:50.280 --> 00:13:52.320
it's really a peer-to-peer

00:13:52.320 --> 00:13:55.830
where people start as a seller
and then also become a buyer

00:13:55.830 --> 00:13:59.170
and it basically fosters
the sense of community.

00:13:59.170 --> 00:14:02.150
But what about the brands in your world?

00:14:02.150 --> 00:14:04.410
Do you have engagement with those brands?

00:14:04.410 --> 00:14:05.243
Yeah.

00:14:05.243 --> 00:14:07.050
So we've been quite lucky actually,

00:14:07.050 --> 00:14:11.920
because we didn't have to go
out there and talk to them.

00:14:11.920 --> 00:14:12.960
They actually came to us,

00:14:12.960 --> 00:14:16.650
initially because they
wanted to get access

00:14:16.650 --> 00:14:18.490
to our community.

00:14:18.490 --> 00:14:19.780
They see, as we see,

00:14:19.780 --> 00:14:23.250
that Gen Z acts and behaves
in a completely different way.

00:14:23.250 --> 00:14:26.270
And so, they wanted to collaborate with us

00:14:26.270 --> 00:14:28.503
to understand that movement a bit better.

00:14:29.480 --> 00:14:32.102
They also want to get
more involved in resell.

00:14:32.102 --> 00:14:35.159
Especially now that has
become much more prominent.

00:14:35.159 --> 00:14:38.940
They're trying to see
what's their place there.

00:14:38.940 --> 00:14:41.580
And so for us, we were super welcoming

00:14:41.580 --> 00:14:43.480
because we know that alone,

00:14:43.480 --> 00:14:46.310
we're not going to change
the world for better.

00:14:46.310 --> 00:14:47.800
So we need to work together.

00:14:47.800 --> 00:14:52.800
How did the pandemic impact
supply on the platform?

00:14:52.950 --> 00:14:55.910
And are there lessons you can learn

00:14:55.910 --> 00:14:58.010
about how to get more
and more people aware

00:14:58.010 --> 00:15:02.593
that actually they have
assets in their wardrobes?

00:15:03.520 --> 00:15:05.460
The pandemic made people realize

00:15:05.460 --> 00:15:08.370
that they had a lot of
things in their closets.

00:15:08.370 --> 00:15:11.540
Marie Kondo was also quite
good on showing people

00:15:11.540 --> 00:15:13.330
that you've got too much.

00:15:13.330 --> 00:15:16.440
I think it was a moment in
time that made people realize,

00:15:16.440 --> 00:15:20.320
wow, this is an issue.

00:15:20.320 --> 00:15:21.153
On the back of that,

00:15:21.153 --> 00:15:23.030
there's obviously a lot
more social consumption.

00:15:23.030 --> 00:15:28.030
So people are realizing that
there's far too much out there.

00:15:28.150 --> 00:15:30.340
If you think about the amount
of items being produced

00:15:30.340 --> 00:15:31.370
on a yearly basis.

00:15:31.370 --> 00:15:32.650
100 billion.

00:15:32.650 --> 00:15:33.600
It's crazy.

00:15:33.600 --> 00:15:38.600
That's like, what more than
250 million items a day.

00:15:38.720 --> 00:15:41.930
So I think people are starting to realize,

00:15:41.930 --> 00:15:45.170
maybe a pandemic had to happen
for people to notice that.

00:15:45.170 --> 00:15:48.390
Supply drove the demands

00:15:48.390 --> 00:15:50.730
because people were
listing all these items,

00:15:50.730 --> 00:15:52.570
they were talking about it in social media

00:15:52.570 --> 00:15:55.003
and then people were
coming to, oh, let's see.

00:15:56.090 --> 00:15:59.210
And so in terms of what we can
learn from that, it's about,

00:15:59.210 --> 00:16:00.550
not mimicking what happened

00:16:00.550 --> 00:16:03.950
because you don't want
people to be at home, right?

00:16:03.950 --> 00:16:06.500
So it's about reducing the frictions

00:16:06.500 --> 00:16:07.740
that they found out on the way.

00:16:07.740 --> 00:16:11.010
So like, how do we make sure
that we connect with buyers

00:16:11.010 --> 00:16:12.100
and sellers in a better way

00:16:12.100 --> 00:16:14.370
so that they don't have to
be looking at multiple items?

00:16:14.370 --> 00:16:16.690
So one of our core areas of work right now

00:16:16.690 --> 00:16:18.780
is personalizing the app

00:16:18.780 --> 00:16:20.170
and really making a big difference

00:16:20.170 --> 00:16:23.610
between the browsing experience
and the search experience.

00:16:23.610 --> 00:16:26.450
Okay, so while all of this craziness

00:16:26.450 --> 00:16:28.283
of a pandemic is going on,

00:16:29.270 --> 00:16:33.680
you announce that you've
been acquired by Etsy.

00:16:33.680 --> 00:16:37.780
What was it? For $1.62 billion?

00:16:37.780 --> 00:16:41.435
So basically two years ago, we met them

00:16:41.435 --> 00:16:45.320
and it was very much like a
knowledge sharing session.

00:16:45.320 --> 00:16:47.440
And some of the things
that we ended up doing was,

00:16:47.440 --> 00:16:49.030
on the back of some of the conversations

00:16:49.030 --> 00:16:49.960
that we had with them,

00:16:49.960 --> 00:16:52.393
I realized that first,

00:16:53.879 --> 00:16:56.720
there's a lot of similarities
in Etsy and Depop,

00:16:56.720 --> 00:16:59.400
in terms of values, if you will.

00:16:59.400 --> 00:17:02.510
So they really, really
foster entrepreneurship

00:17:02.510 --> 00:17:03.850
and creativity.

00:17:03.850 --> 00:17:07.160
And they also really believe in consumers

00:17:07.160 --> 00:17:08.453
that are community led.

00:17:11.570 --> 00:17:14.690
More than 2 million users
joined the Depop community

00:17:14.690 --> 00:17:16.060
in 2020,

00:17:16.060 --> 00:17:19.563
proving the rapid trajectory
of the fashion resale market.

00:17:21.160 --> 00:17:23.761
I met with Fiona Short and Youth,

00:17:23.761 --> 00:17:26.659
two long time Depop sellers.

00:17:26.659 --> 00:17:27.710
Nice to meet you.

00:17:27.710 --> 00:17:28.543
How are you?

00:17:28.543 --> 00:17:30.110
I've heard all about you.

00:17:30.110 --> 00:17:31.890
Both have built their own businesses

00:17:31.890 --> 00:17:34.850
to capitalize on this opportunity.

00:17:34.850 --> 00:17:36.600
Now you're both in this market

00:17:36.600 --> 00:17:40.370
that they're calling the
fashion resale market,

00:17:40.370 --> 00:17:43.660
and you guys are Depop sellers,

00:17:43.660 --> 00:17:45.500
how do you define the market?

00:17:45.500 --> 00:17:46.340
Within the community,

00:17:46.340 --> 00:17:51.110
we don't tend to use the
word resellers for ourselves

00:17:51.110 --> 00:17:52.980
because what we do is
much more than reselling.

00:17:52.980 --> 00:17:55.444
It's curating, sometimes
you're fixing pieces.

00:17:55.444 --> 00:17:58.000
In fact, some of the pieces that we buy

00:17:58.000 --> 00:17:59.070
are dead stock pieces,

00:17:59.070 --> 00:18:01.610
and haven't even been
sold to a consumer before.

00:18:01.610 --> 00:18:04.940
The term resellers, we use
more for the drop culture.

00:18:04.940 --> 00:18:08.220
So we just tend to call
ourselves vintage sellers

00:18:08.220 --> 00:18:09.780
or vintage dealers.

00:18:09.780 --> 00:18:12.910
Yeah, there's so much to this industry

00:18:12.910 --> 00:18:15.330
or whatever you want to
call it, this community.

00:18:15.330 --> 00:18:18.380
I feel like you can have
so many different interests

00:18:18.380 --> 00:18:20.410
and whatever you do day to day,

00:18:20.410 --> 00:18:22.460
whether it be photography or styling,

00:18:22.460 --> 00:18:24.460
or whether you like hiking,

00:18:24.460 --> 00:18:27.440
they always come through
in your vintage curation.

00:18:27.440 --> 00:18:30.900
So even though we do resell,
and that is a big part of it

00:18:30.900 --> 00:18:33.970
and it keeps us going and
it allows us to carry on.

00:18:33.970 --> 00:18:35.650
I think the main core part of it

00:18:35.650 --> 00:18:37.540
is who we really are day to day

00:18:37.540 --> 00:18:40.439
and what inspires us and our experiences,

00:18:40.439 --> 00:18:43.370
where we come from and
what we really stand for.

00:18:43.370 --> 00:18:45.875
Okay. So I have a little
challenge for you.

00:18:45.875 --> 00:18:46.708
Okay.

00:18:46.708 --> 00:18:48.540
I bought this t-shirt,

00:18:48.540 --> 00:18:51.140
it's the first thing I ever
bought on the internet.

00:18:51.140 --> 00:18:51.973
Wow.

00:18:51.973 --> 00:18:52.806
Okay.

00:18:52.806 --> 00:18:54.690
Like the first piece of
clothing I ever bought

00:18:54.690 --> 00:18:55.523
on the internet.

00:18:55.523 --> 00:18:58.320
I think I bought it in 1999 or 2000.

00:18:58.320 --> 00:18:59.960
Would this have any value?

00:18:59.960 --> 00:19:02.060
If this was from like a concert or a tour

00:19:02.060 --> 00:19:03.240
or something like this,

00:19:03.240 --> 00:19:05.650
the price of this would be a lot higher.

00:19:05.650 --> 00:19:07.220
So this is double stitching,

00:19:07.220 --> 00:19:09.270
which means it's made after 1992.

00:19:09.270 --> 00:19:11.690
But if it's single stitch,
then it's before 1992,

00:19:11.690 --> 00:19:13.720
which means it's going to
be worth a lot more money.

00:19:13.720 --> 00:19:16.680
Can you show me the kind of
stuff that you guys sell?

00:19:16.680 --> 00:19:18.282
Yes, of course.
Absolutely.

00:19:18.282 --> 00:19:20.690
On Depop, and explain
to us why it's valuable.

00:19:20.690 --> 00:19:23.990
This is actually the very first piece

00:19:24.980 --> 00:19:28.193
that I picked up from
the markets in Italy.

00:19:29.067 --> 00:19:31.212
€1, €1 everybody.

00:19:31.212 --> 00:19:32.657
€1.

00:19:32.657 --> 00:19:36.727
€1. It's a John Galliano top, right?

00:19:37.624 --> 00:19:38.457
It's just a top.

00:19:38.457 --> 00:19:39.290
Is it a bathing suit?

00:19:39.290 --> 00:19:40.123
It's a bathing suit.

00:19:40.123 --> 00:19:44.060
Beach wear, I believe
it's from the early 2000s,

00:19:44.060 --> 00:19:46.740
newspaper print, iconic John Galliano.

00:19:46.740 --> 00:19:49.254
And how much could that sell for?

00:19:49.254 --> 00:19:52.040
I mean, you know more about John Galliano,

00:19:52.040 --> 00:19:54.450
but, I believe £110, maybe £90.

00:19:54.450 --> 00:19:55.740
Maybe, yeah, yeah.

00:19:55.740 --> 00:19:57.660
All right, Youth. What about you?

00:19:57.660 --> 00:19:59.330
I bought this at a market

00:20:00.921 --> 00:20:03.680
and it's a Betty Boop Versace print,

00:20:03.680 --> 00:20:06.990
which is one of Gianni
Versace really iconic prints.

00:20:06.990 --> 00:20:08.560
What year do you think this is from?

00:20:08.560 --> 00:20:12.150
This is like late 90s.
It's got double stitching.

00:20:12.150 --> 00:20:14.040
There was one that was like early 90s,

00:20:14.040 --> 00:20:16.770
which was single stitching,
but this is a later version,

00:20:16.770 --> 00:20:19.920
but this has come back
into pop culture through,

00:20:19.920 --> 00:20:24.910
I think Cardi B wore an all in
one Versace with this print.

00:20:24.910 --> 00:20:27.860
And it's Betty Boop and a guest, Madonna.

00:20:27.860 --> 00:20:31.510
So where does this market go from here?

00:20:31.510 --> 00:20:34.880
How do you see the
resell market developing

00:20:34.880 --> 00:20:37.860
and how might that
impact the way you manage

00:20:37.860 --> 00:20:40.570
and run your own businesses?

00:20:40.570 --> 00:20:43.010
Personally, I see this already expanding.

00:20:43.010 --> 00:20:45.380
There's people who are
starting in their bedroom,

00:20:45.380 --> 00:20:46.880
selling that own clothes.

00:20:46.880 --> 00:20:50.340
And gradually now going into wholesaling.

00:20:50.340 --> 00:20:53.290
As the industry does get more saturated

00:20:53.290 --> 00:20:55.910
and people do want to get
involved in different ways,

00:20:55.910 --> 00:20:58.300
there are obviously
windows that are opening

00:20:58.300 --> 00:20:59.849
and doors that are opening.

00:20:59.849 --> 00:21:00.682
Yeah.

00:21:00.682 --> 00:21:02.520
I think one of the things
that I like so much

00:21:02.520 --> 00:21:04.030
about this industry,

00:21:04.030 --> 00:21:06.810
is that it's based on independent sellers,

00:21:06.810 --> 00:21:08.810
individuals that are in their bedroom.

00:21:08.810 --> 00:21:10.660
The new thing isn't now
to get a paper round,

00:21:10.660 --> 00:21:12.797
it's to get your clothes on Depop.

00:21:12.797 --> 00:21:14.760
And I think that we're going to see

00:21:14.760 --> 00:21:17.440
so many young, young, young people

00:21:17.440 --> 00:21:20.420
are starting their businesses
and growing really fast.

00:21:20.420 --> 00:21:22.400
I think that it's just going to become

00:21:22.400 --> 00:21:24.370
even more of a social norm

00:21:24.370 --> 00:21:25.560
because remember like 10 years ago

00:21:25.560 --> 00:21:27.580
when secondhand clothes wasn't cool.

00:21:27.580 --> 00:21:28.860
It was counter-culture.

00:21:28.860 --> 00:21:32.528
But now, as the coin's flipped
and it's became mainstream,

00:21:32.528 --> 00:21:35.940
Fiona sold to Bella Hadid before.

00:21:35.940 --> 00:21:36.773
You have?

00:21:36.773 --> 00:21:37.606
Yeah.

00:21:37.606 --> 00:21:38.439
What did she buy?

00:21:38.439 --> 00:21:41.400
She bought two really
beautiful 90s vintage tops.

00:21:41.400 --> 00:21:43.930
They were really reasonably
priced. They were beautiful.

00:21:43.930 --> 00:21:46.700
They were just on the Depop,
available to everybody.

00:21:46.700 --> 00:21:49.328
And I didn't even know
it was actually to Bella.

00:21:49.328 --> 00:21:52.265
And then I saw it on her Instagram.

00:21:52.265 --> 00:21:53.545
She was wearing it.

00:21:53.545 --> 00:21:54.378
Yeah.

00:21:54.378 --> 00:21:56.620
She could get anything she wanted

00:21:56.620 --> 00:22:00.390
and she actually sought products out

00:22:00.390 --> 00:22:02.029
that she couldn't get anywhere else.

00:22:02.029 --> 00:22:02.862
Yeah.

00:22:02.862 --> 00:22:03.695
That's really cool.

00:22:03.695 --> 00:22:04.528
Yeah.

00:22:08.710 --> 00:22:10.070
Well, the most interesting thing for me

00:22:10.070 --> 00:22:13.340
is that people are
building real businesses

00:22:13.340 --> 00:22:16.050
and livelihoods on these platforms.

00:22:16.050 --> 00:22:19.370
They've created these whole
new models of interacting

00:22:19.370 --> 00:22:20.760
with customers.

00:22:20.760 --> 00:22:22.630
In the next 10, 15, 20 years,

00:22:22.630 --> 00:22:24.850
as consumers become more comfortable,

00:22:24.850 --> 00:22:27.580
and these platforms grow in visibility,

00:22:27.580 --> 00:22:28.990
I think it's really going to transform

00:22:28.990 --> 00:22:32.800
the way a lot of customers
engage with the fashion business.

00:22:32.800 --> 00:22:33.810
It's not going away.

00:22:33.810 --> 00:22:36.630
The market is going to grow massively

00:22:36.630 --> 00:22:37.860
over the next 10 years.

00:22:37.860 --> 00:22:39.830
There's huge amounts of inventory

00:22:39.830 --> 00:22:41.430
sitting in all of our closets.

00:22:41.430 --> 00:22:43.930
It's just about getting that
inventory and circulation,

00:22:43.930 --> 00:22:46.780
getting brands to accept that
this is a permanent fixture

00:22:46.780 --> 00:22:49.775
of the market going forward
and increasing the visibility

00:22:49.775 --> 00:22:52.423
of these platforms amongst customers.

